I. Check your CLISP

You should have Common LISP installed. The necessary version is
2.39 of 2006-07-16 or a newer one.

On your Windows desktop, select Srart | Run (the Start button is
usually located in the lower left corner of the screen).
Enter the command 
  cmd
if you are under Windows NT/2000/2003/XP, and
  command 
under Windows 95/98/ME.
    
At the terminal window, try to issue the command
  clisp
  
If the CLISP installation directory is not in PATH, issue:
  <CLISP-dir>\clisp
  
The directory <CLISP-dir> contains the executable clisp.exe, 
documents like README and NEWS, and a lot of subdirectories:
base, bindings, ..., full, ..., src, ... 
  
If CLISP starts, issue the command:
  (lisp-implementation-version)
The result should be like this:    
  [1]> (lisp-implementation-version)
  "2.39 (2006-07-16) (built on stnt067 [192.168.0.1])"
  [2]>_

Then enter
  (quit)
to terminate the CLISP session.

Issue the command
  exit
to terminate the terminal session and to close the terminal
window.  

If CLISP does not start, ask you system administrator to get 
the newest CLISP from 
  http://clisp.sourceforge.net/ 
and to install it globally for all users. Add <CLISP-dir>
to the PATH environment variable to start CLISP by a simple command
  clisp

II. Installation

Start the terminal (cmd or command), change to directory
<bergman-root-dir>\scripts\clisp\win9598nt, and issue the
installation command

The syntax of the installation command is:
  makebr.bat <CLISP-dir>

For example, if you have Common Lisp installed in the directory 
c:\clisp-2.39 and unpacked Bergman in the directory 
d:\bergman0.99 then you may install Bergman from the directory
d:\bergman0.99\scripts\clisp\win9598nt using the following command:
  makebr.bat c:\clisp-2.39

After installation issue the command
  exit
to terminate the terminal session and to close the terminal
window.  

III. Log file

  Log of installation is <bergman-root-dir>/logs/clwin9598nt.log.  
